{
 "total": 30.04,
 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "text": "There's a metal rod inside your water heater that is slowly sacrificing itself.",
   "start": 0.25,
   "end": 4.523
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "text": "It's called the anode rod. Magnesium, hanging from the top of the tank.",
   "start": 4.703,
   "end": 8.906
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "text": "Water corrodes metal, but it attacks the more reactive metal first.",
   "start": 9.086,
   "end": 13.892
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "text": "So the rod dissolves, instead of your steel tank.",
   "start": 14.072,
   "end": 16.772
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "text": "After about five years, it's mostly gone.",
   "start": 16.952,
   "end": 19.688
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "text": "Then the water starts eating the tank itself. That's when leaks begin.",
   "start": 19.868,
   "end": 24.095
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "text": "A new rod costs about twenty dollars. A new heater, over a thousand.",
   "start": 24.275,
   "end": 28.66
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "text": "Check yours.",
   "start": 28.84,
   "end": 29.44
  }
 ],
